Q: Rescale SVG to a Target size A: <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> You can easily use your current SVG e.g. for a 64x64px target size by applying CSS width/height property values.
No need to recalculate the intrinsic coordinates.</p>
<h3>Scaling SVG is usually not necessary</h3>
<p>The main benefit of scalable vector graphics – the ultimate display size is <em>mostly</em> irrelevant.<br />
Recalculating the actual SVG command coordinates to a target size also usually doesn't have significant benefits regarding file size.</p>
<p>However there are exceptions:</p>
<h4>When scaling SVG coordinates makes sense</h4>
<p>your native command coordinate space is extremely</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>large:</strong> CAD applications tend to store/export vector coordinates in real-life physical units with an &quot;insane&quot; precision. This precision makes sense for applications like CNC/Laser-plotting or 3D printing applications but are an overkill for graphic presentation</li>
<li>or <strong>small:</strong> GeoJSON data is based on physical latitudes and longitudes. When we retrieve polygon vertices for a city or country we're mostly dealing with boundaries smaller than 1 user unit. Sometimes we may even scratch the numerical limits of browser renderers</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common practices for SVG icon assets</h3>
<ul>
<li>FontAwesome uses an artboard size of up to 512x512 pixels, which allows for the use of integer coordinate values.</li>
<li>Material, Feather or Heroicons icons (and many other libraries) prefer a 24x24 reference size – in case of Material icons heavily depending on a sufficient floating point accuracy. Some more complex shapes simply can't be represented with integer only SVG command coordinates without introducing ugly distortions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The perfect <code>viewBox</code>?</h3>
<p>Your example shows a typical icon usage - quite likely designed in Adobe Illustrator.
The icon is based on a 1000x1000 <code>viewBox</code> or reference frame – in a graphic editor this would be your artboard/document size.</p>
<p>A 1000x1000 viewBox usually allows integer coordinates – <strong>trivia</strong> CFF OpenType fonts traditionally used this coordinate space recommendation for glyphs/character shapes sufficient even for delicate curvatures.</p>
<p>But as commented by <em>enxaneta</em> you may consider to decrease the whitespace around the current icon for a tighter bounding box.</p>
<p>However a certain ammount of whitespace is usually desired to avoid icons to bump into adjacent text-elements (such as labels).</p>
<h4>Consistent design concepts and grids</h4>
<p>Ideally you should avoid mixing different design grids or icon libraries simply because it complicates the coding process e.g when applying stroke widths.</p>
<p>For instance it becomes quite inconvenient to normalize stroke widths across multiple different viewBox sizes:</p>
<ul>
<li>icons based on a 24x24 unit grid may work best with <code>stroke-width:2px</code></li>
<li>when based on a 1000x1000 space you probably need a &quot;clunkier&quot; CSS value like <code>16.47px</code></li>
</ul>
<h3>Should I prefer 24x24 as well?</h3>
<p>There is no magic behind it. The main reason many icon design libraries have opted for a 24x24 reference frame is the fact that a 24 grid is quite convenient (divisible by 2, 3, 4, 6) and 24px also happens to be a reasonably readable display size for UI elements.</p>
<p>Most importantly, if you &quot;abide&quot; to a 24px display size and all important contours are aligned to this 24x24 grid – there is a good chance the ultimate rendering get's as crisp as it gets (so avoiding visible antialiasing artifacts/interpolation which may make your rendering appear blurry).</p>
<p>However, it won't ensure your SVG graphics will always render perfectly crisp!</p>
<h3>Keep as it is: »[...] without editing all the coordinates«?</h3>
<p>As previously explained, the native coordinate space shouldn't be the problem to get the desired rendering...</p>
<p>However, you most likely encounter issues when using SVGs directly as exported by graphic applications as they:</p>
<ul>
<li>usually have a lot of bloat e.g meta-data are not great in terms of file-size</li>
<li>overhead/metadata useless for web applications</li>
<li>unnecessary style properties - e.g for default values like <code>fill:black</code> that could be omitted</li>
<li>overly specific style properties in form of inline styles which makes overriding these contextually unnecessarily hard</li>
<li>depending on your SVG embedding method: the generic Adobe Illustrator CSS class names can be problematic when inlining multiple assets to the DOM: a subsequent <code>.cls-1</code> will override previous definitions</li>
</ul>
<p>Here's an example of an &quot;optimized&quot; SVG</p>
